/* 
What is memory?
- Whenever Javascript runs a program it need memory to store the information so that program can use them.

Two Memory concepts: 
Stack Memory
Heap Memory

Stack Memory: 
- Stack memory actually stores the primitive values.
- The primitive value is copied 

Ex:
let a = 10      // original value
let b = a       // Copies value of a
b = 50          // Updating b doesn't change a

console.log(b)  // 50
console.log(a)  // 10
*/

/* 
HEAP MEMEORY

- Heap memory actually stores the Non-primitive object/values.
- Object itself stores in the heap
- The variable name holds the reference of that object to point to it.

let userOne = {name: "Keshava"}
let userTwo = userOne
userTwo.name = "Rahul"      // Copies the reference, not the object

console.log(userOne.name)   // "Rahul" - both variable refer to the same object
*/